the  sequencing annotation and discovery of  the human genome and the genes that are  responsible for different proteins to  technologies that make such data easy to  produce  and eventually to use  in large-scale initiatives like genomics  england  the broad institute  different  medical centers that actually use  insights from genomic data to categorize  diagnose and treat patients  now the basis of that is that  when we understand the code and we  understand what the code actually uh  produces so how nucleotide sequences  form amino acid sequences that are the  basis of proteins we can start looking  at variation of that dna code that is  responsible for some specific phenotype  or in this case in this example you can  see sickle cell anemia actually a change  in a particular  amino acid leads to a change in  a cell structure right  and so  that kind of an ability to  detect changes  has led to a number of very clear  applications so for example drug  metabolism how different individuals  metabolize different drugs and whether  they will respond or not respond to  treatment  identification of mutations in cancer  cells identifying genetic predisposition  or disorders like sickle cell  anemia inherited mutations  rare genetic diseases  as well as drug resistance and different  pathogens and these examples are all in  the context of biomedical research and  medical application in the context of  precision or personalized medicine  because what happens in the medical  context is today clinicians have to deal  with such information to actually make  a better um description or  characterization of disease for a  patient and and one of the things that  we now are starting to see is that you  have these panels of  specific genes specific  proteins  and  you can now identify mutations that are  responsible for  very dangerous diseases like cancer now  as researchers continue to study these  they actually understand that  some specific genes in the genome are  responsible for the most  serious effects so for example tumor  suppressor genes on protein genes dna  repair genes they accumulate mutations  that are most dangerous one example is  the tp-53 mutation that  is a gene p53 protein  tumor protein p53 is encoded in that  gene and what that mutation does is it  can actually alter the structure of that  protein and make it not functional and  if it's not functional it's a tumor  suppressor gene so then it does not  suppress the tumor growth because it  does not respond to the damage in the  dna and leads to accumulation of  mutations so finding such mutations can  be critical for accurate and precise and  effective cancer cancer treatment  in cystic fibrosis  you have  mutations that are responsible for the  functioning of  the cftr channel  which is responsible for  getting the right kind of uh  chloride ions out of the cells and if  it's not functioning it leads to  accumulation of mucus and can be very  dangerous so knowing how to find these  mutations and understand their function  is clearly important and not only is  this important for individual mutations  this is also important for large scale  rearrangements like copy number  variation  repeats that expand a particular region  and and one way to find those variations  is to perform genome-wide analyses that  take thousands of samples for clear  phenotypes and start understanding what  are the statistically significant  patterns that occur in a large  proportion of the population  such studies have led to discoveries  including tuberculosis drug resistance  which is a disease that is affecting  millions of individuals around the world  if someone can monitor the participants  and make sure that no one  think if you could keep them unmuted  thank you  so drug resistance  not only to individual drugs but  multiple drugs is starting to become a  huge problem  and and we see the same in the  world of viral pathogens so for example  the ebola outbreak and how that leads to  tracking of transmission and vaccine  design but also the current pandemic of  covert 19 we hear about variance of  concern all the time so all of these  project examples all of these  you know examples in precision medicine  in population health  genetic predisposition to disease all of  these have been transformed by the  availability of these different types of  sequencing techniques so in the program  what we will try to do is introduce you  to how such data is generated  what is the process of acquiring actual  digital data from a biological sample  and then in that data set how do we work  with  different algorithms computational  algorithms to understand mutations that  are pathogenic neutral  that are maybe not very bad or bad  and how do we determine the effect of  these mutations in the genome so how do  we actually  categorize them how do we understand  their impact on the phenotype and what  are some examples where you can find uh  the you know clear association between  individual mutations and the phenotype  and so that is really what  bioinformatics is all about so  bioinformatics is about taking  information or data  taking methods of computational analysis  statistical methods machine learning  analytical methods and applying them to  biological data sets applying them to  data sets where we have biological  samples conditions clinical conditions  etc and so the program is going to  tie together these three different  domains  in a way that you can quickly see the  application of bioinformatics to a  number of different domains so the  omixlogic  programs are going to help you not only  understand how these things work but  also practice and apply them and so in  the context of this program these  resources that were already mentioned by  sri gauri are going to be critical to  getting you from maybe not knowing a lot  about bioinformatics to getting an  understanding of what bioinformatics is  and how it applies to eventually  starting to use it maybe i